WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.290 --> 00:00:01.350
- If you were following the trial

00:00:01.350 --> 00:00:03.150
of Kyle Rittenhouse on social media,

00:00:03.150 --> 00:00:04.570
you might've come to the conclusion

00:00:04.570 --> 00:00:07.870
that Judge Schroeder was a
hopelessly biased nutcase

00:00:07.870 --> 00:00:09.310
who was making up all the rules

00:00:09.310 --> 00:00:11.590
to help Kyle Rittenhouse.

00:00:11.590 --> 00:00:12.473
Was he?

00:00:12.473 --> 00:00:15.056
(gentle music)

00:00:16.440 --> 00:00:17.530
Now most of the situations

00:00:17.530 --> 00:00:19.180
that people are objecting to involve

00:00:19.180 --> 00:00:20.910
the application of the rules of evidence.

00:00:20.910 --> 00:00:22.980
In state court, the judge
has broad discretion

00:00:22.980 --> 00:00:25.110
to make rulings on the
admissibility of evidence

00:00:25.110 --> 00:00:26.810
and how to run their courtroom.

00:00:26.810 --> 00:00:28.500
This includes things like which witnesses

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.500
are allowed to testify,

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.260
what the lawyers are allowed to ask,

00:00:31.260 --> 00:00:33.040
what topics are off limits,

00:00:33.040 --> 00:00:34.980
and a whole bunch of other things.

00:00:34.980 --> 00:00:37.330
And there were a few
incidents that stuck out.

00:00:37.330 --> 00:00:38.750
So let's talk about incident number one,

00:00:38.750 --> 00:00:39.920
where Judge Schroeder ruled

00:00:39.920 --> 00:00:41.500
that the prosecution
could not call the people

00:00:41.500 --> 00:00:44.590
that Kyle Rittenhouse
shot and killed victims.

00:00:44.590 --> 00:00:45.640
The defense attorneys argue

00:00:45.640 --> 00:00:47.010
that using the word victim

00:00:47.010 --> 00:00:49.180
could prejudice the jury
against Rittenhouse.

00:00:49.180 --> 00:00:50.640
Judge Schroeder said, "The word victim

00:00:50.640 --> 00:00:52.470
is a loaded, loaded word."

00:00:52.470 --> 00:00:54.530
When the judge ruled in
favor of the defendant,

00:00:54.530 --> 00:00:55.760
critics jumped to the conclusion

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:56.940
that Judge Schroeder was showing

00:00:56.940 --> 00:00:59.090
inappropriate favoritism to Rittenhouse.

00:00:59.090 --> 00:01:01.360
Now trial judges have lots of discretion.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:02.700
The Wisconsin rules of evidence

00:01:02.700 --> 00:01:04.470
mostly track the federal
rules of evidence.

00:01:04.470 --> 00:01:06.810
Even if particular evidence
isn't explicitly ruled out,

00:01:06.810 --> 00:01:08.400
judges have lots of leeway.

00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:10.860
Wisconsin rule of evidence 904.03,

00:01:10.860 --> 00:01:11.920
which is very similar

00:01:11.920 --> 00:01:14.397
to federal rule of evidence 403 states,

00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:16.240
"Although relevant,
evidence may be excluded

00:01:16.240 --> 00:01:18.280
if its probative value is
substantially outweighed

00:01:18.280 --> 00:01:19.890
by the danger of unfair prejudice,

00:01:19.890 --> 00:01:22.200
confusion of the issues,
or misleading the jury,

00:01:22.200 --> 00:01:23.810
or by considerations of undue delay,

00:01:23.810 --> 00:01:25.480
waste of time, or needless presentation

00:01:25.480 --> 00:01:26.810
of cumulative evidence."

00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:29.200
In other words, evidence
might be minimally relevant,

00:01:29.200 --> 00:01:31.610
but if the risk of prejudice is too great,

00:01:31.610 --> 00:01:34.130
you shouldn't allow that
evidence into the courtroom.

00:01:34.130 --> 00:01:36.520
And the way that the victim
is used in a courtroom

00:01:36.520 --> 00:01:38.640
is different than what's used
outside of the courthouse.

00:01:38.640 --> 00:01:40.920
Rittenhouse claimed that he
was acting in self-defense.

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:42.450
If he was right, the three men

00:01:42.450 --> 00:01:43.770
would not be considered victims

00:01:43.770 --> 00:01:44.940
in the legal sense of the word.

00:01:44.940 --> 00:01:47.150
They would be assailants
who attacked Rittenhouse.

00:01:47.150 --> 00:01:48.930
People also suggested that Judge Schroeder

00:01:48.930 --> 00:01:50.960
was making a special rules
just for Rittenhouse,

00:01:50.960 --> 00:01:52.730
but the judge's ruling was consistent

00:01:52.730 --> 00:01:54.530
with the way that he
handles criminal trials.

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.327
According to the Kenosha News,

00:01:56.327 --> 00:01:57.980
"that is Schroeder standard practice

00:01:57.980 --> 00:01:59.490
in criminal cases and is not unique

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.540
to this handling of the Rittenhouse case."

00:02:01.540 --> 00:02:03.270
Criminal lawyers said these rulings

00:02:03.270 --> 00:02:05.310
were commonplace in their
jurisdictions as well.

00:02:05.310 --> 00:02:07.800
Victims suggest guilt before it is proven

00:02:07.800 --> 00:02:10.480
and prosecuting and
convicting someone is hard,

00:02:10.480 --> 00:02:12.010
as well it should be.

00:02:12.010 --> 00:02:13.870
And of course, it's always okay to refer

00:02:13.870 --> 00:02:15.170
to these people by their names,

00:02:15.170 --> 00:02:16.660
which tactically can be even better

00:02:16.660 --> 00:02:17.830
because it humanizes them.

00:02:17.830 --> 00:02:19.300
In a court of law, a criminal defendant

00:02:19.300 --> 00:02:21.540
must be given his full due process rights.

00:02:21.540 --> 00:02:22.900
The whole object of a fair trial

00:02:22.900 --> 00:02:24.850
is not to prejudge the defendant.

00:02:24.850 --> 00:02:26.437
That means judges should
be skeptical of evidence

00:02:26.437 --> 00:02:29.440
that a jury could use to
infer someone's guilt,

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.040
if that evidence is not truly
probative of what happened.

00:02:32.040 --> 00:02:33.470
And that brings us to the second incidence

00:02:33.470 --> 00:02:34.380
where Judge Schroeder ruled

00:02:34.380 --> 00:02:35.950
that the three men who were shot

00:02:35.950 --> 00:02:38.550
could be referred to as
rioters, looters, and arsonists.

00:02:38.550 --> 00:02:40.200
Well, specifically what
Judge Schroeder said

00:02:40.200 --> 00:02:42.210
was let the evidence show
what the evidence shows,

00:02:42.210 --> 00:02:44.270
that any or one of these people

00:02:44.270 --> 00:02:46.370
were engaged in arson,
rioting, or looting,

00:02:46.370 --> 00:02:47.900
that I'm not going to tell the defense

00:02:47.900 --> 00:02:49.190
they can't call them that.

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:50.310
Prosecutor Thomas Binger said

00:02:50.310 --> 00:02:51.537
that this was a double standard.

00:02:51.537 --> 00:02:52.900
"I think it's the exact same issue.

00:02:52.900 --> 00:02:54.280
The terms that I'm identifying here,

00:02:54.280 --> 00:02:56.120
such as rioters, looters, arsonists,

00:02:56.120 --> 00:02:58.620
are as loaded, if not more
loaded, than the term victim."

00:02:58.620 --> 00:03:00.920
First, a defendant
generally gets to put on

00:03:00.920 --> 00:03:02.220
whatever defense they want.

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.390
So a court should err on the side

00:03:04.390 --> 00:03:06.200
of allowing a defendant to do something

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:09.030
and get more freedom than the prosecution.

00:03:09.030 --> 00:03:11.030
But second of all, Judge
Schroeder did not rule

00:03:11.030 --> 00:03:12.950
that the defense could
absolutely call them

00:03:12.950 --> 00:03:13.910
rioters and looters.

00:03:13.910 --> 00:03:15.850
He ruled that if the defense could prove

00:03:15.850 --> 00:03:18.350
that the three men were
rioting and looting,

00:03:18.350 --> 00:03:19.630
then they could use those terms

00:03:19.630 --> 00:03:20.930
during the closing argument.

00:03:20.930 --> 00:03:22.340
And the judge prohibited the defense

00:03:22.340 --> 00:03:24.640
from using those terms during
their opening statement,

00:03:24.640 --> 00:03:27.210
very similar to the ruling he
made about the word victim.

00:03:27.210 --> 00:03:28.520
Neither side could use those terms

00:03:28.520 --> 00:03:30.100
unless they prove their case.

00:03:30.100 --> 00:03:31.690
That takes us to incident number three,

00:03:31.690 --> 00:03:33.470
where Judge Schroeder dressed down

00:03:33.470 --> 00:03:34.447
the prosecutor saying,

00:03:34.447 --> 00:03:36.570
"Don't get brazen with me!"

00:03:36.570 --> 00:03:38.460
Judge Schroeder was again accused of bias

00:03:38.460 --> 00:03:40.300
when he blew up at
Prosecutor Thomas Binger.

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:43.010
First of all, yes,
judges do yell at lawyers

00:03:43.010 --> 00:03:45.380
and sometimes those lawyers deserve it.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:46.980
Some do it more frequently than others.

00:03:46.980 --> 00:03:48.560
And Kenosha lawyers say that Schroeder

00:03:48.560 --> 00:03:50.300
is definitely a yeller.

00:03:50.300 --> 00:03:52.440
Now second, the judge sent
the jury out of the room

00:03:52.440 --> 00:03:53.900
before admonishing the prosecutor.

00:03:53.900 --> 00:03:55.760
And third, and most importantly,

00:03:55.760 --> 00:03:58.120
we should look to what caused
Schroeder to blow his top.

00:03:58.120 --> 00:03:59.520
When Rittenhouse took to the stand

00:03:59.520 --> 00:04:00.710
to testify in his own defense,

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:01.980
the prosecutor seemed to refer

00:04:01.980 --> 00:04:04.657
to Rittenhouse's prior
silence about what happened.

00:04:04.657 --> 00:04:06.200
"Since August 25th, 2020,

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.580
this is the first time that
you have told your story."

00:04:08.580 --> 00:04:10.640
That's what the prosecutor
asked on the stand.

00:04:10.640 --> 00:04:11.910
The defense immediately objected

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:13.450
and Schroeder sustained the objection,

00:04:13.450 --> 00:04:14.650
disallowing the question.

00:04:14.650 --> 00:04:15.840
And there's good reason for that.

00:04:15.840 --> 00:04:18.010
In criminal cases, the
5th Amendment protects

00:04:18.010 --> 00:04:20.460
against self-incrimination,
among other things.

00:04:20.460 --> 00:04:23.650
This means that a defendant's
decision not to talk

00:04:23.650 --> 00:04:25.630
can't be used against them.

00:04:25.630 --> 00:04:26.950
The judge sustained the objection

00:04:26.950 --> 00:04:29.560
because Binger's question
was too close to the line.

00:04:29.560 --> 00:04:30.920
But then Binger basically asked

00:04:30.920 --> 00:04:32.670
the same question a different way.

00:04:32.670 --> 00:04:34.050
He referred to the many months of people

00:04:34.050 --> 00:04:35.527
talking about the case and said,

00:04:35.527 --> 00:04:36.620
"After all of that now,

00:04:36.620 --> 00:04:39.350
you are telling us your
side of the story, correct?"

00:04:39.350 --> 00:04:40.900
It seems that what Binger was getting at

00:04:40.900 --> 00:04:43.670
was that because Rittenhouse
hadn't testified before

00:04:43.670 --> 00:04:46.260
and was now testifying after everyone else

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:48.720
had taken the stand, and
after all of the evidence

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:50.540
had come out, that Rittenhouse was now

00:04:50.540 --> 00:04:52.410
in a position where he
could change his story

00:04:52.410 --> 00:04:53.690
and craft it to be consistent

00:04:53.690 --> 00:04:55.470
with everyone else's testimony.

00:04:55.470 --> 00:04:58.700
But, of course, a question
can be two things at once.

00:04:58.700 --> 00:05:00.150
And if it's too prejudicial,

00:05:00.150 --> 00:05:02.380
even if there is some
probative value to it,

00:05:02.380 --> 00:05:04.500
well, then the court
can still disallow it,

00:05:04.500 --> 00:05:06.880
which the court specifically had done it.

00:05:06.880 --> 00:05:07.840
Schroeder had disallowed it.

00:05:07.840 --> 00:05:10.250
So Judge Schroeder asked
to the jury be removed

00:05:10.250 --> 00:05:12.910
so that he could basically
just yell at Binger.

00:05:12.910 --> 00:05:14.460
- You need to account for this.

00:05:15.340 --> 00:05:19.100
The problem is, this is a
grave constitutional violation

00:05:19.100 --> 00:05:24.093
for you to talk about
the defendant's silence.

00:05:25.240 --> 00:05:27.993
And that is, and you're right,

00:05:29.350 --> 00:05:32.473
you're right on the borderline.

00:05:32.473 --> 00:05:35.720
And you may be over.

00:05:35.720 --> 00:05:37.370
But it better stop.

00:05:37.370 --> 00:05:38.590
- In other words, it doesn't matter

00:05:38.590 --> 00:05:40.240
whether you think
Rittenhouse was guilty or not

00:05:40.240 --> 00:05:41.860
in the context of a criminal trial,

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.360
his post-arrest silence
couldn't be used against him.

00:05:44.360 --> 00:05:45.370
Binger said he understood,

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:47.210
but then to add insult to injury,

00:05:47.210 --> 00:05:49.040
he then plunged into a series of questions

00:05:49.040 --> 00:05:51.100
about a matter the judge
had already previously ruled

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:52.120
was off limits,

00:05:52.120 --> 00:05:54.060
a video where Rittenhouse fantasized

00:05:54.060 --> 00:05:56.250
about shooting alleged shoplifters,

00:05:56.250 --> 00:05:58.060
which takes us incident number four.

00:05:58.060 --> 00:05:59.330
The judge ruled that the prosecution

00:05:59.330 --> 00:06:01.310
could not show a video
showing Rittenhouse saying

00:06:01.310 --> 00:06:03.180
that he wished he could shoot two people

00:06:03.180 --> 00:06:04.550
leaving a CVS store.

00:06:04.550 --> 00:06:06.190
Now the background is that before trial,

00:06:06.190 --> 00:06:08.060
the prosecution moved
to introduce evidence

00:06:08.060 --> 00:06:09.450
of a video where Rittenhouse says

00:06:09.450 --> 00:06:11.060
he wished he could shoot two people

00:06:11.060 --> 00:06:12.800
who were suspected shoplifters.

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.420
The prosecutor said the video was relevant

00:06:14.420 --> 00:06:16.070
to Rittenhouse's state of mind and motive.

00:06:16.070 --> 00:06:19.240
He said, Rittenhouse was
"essentially a teenage vigilante

00:06:19.240 --> 00:06:21.640
involving himself in things
that don't concern him."

00:06:21.640 --> 00:06:23.270
Now the judge excluded the video

00:06:23.270 --> 00:06:24.190
on the grounds that the video

00:06:24.190 --> 00:06:25.630
wasn't made before the riots.

00:06:25.630 --> 00:06:27.320
It was recorded two weeks earlier.

00:06:27.320 --> 00:06:28.780
And the judge felt that in another video,

00:06:28.780 --> 00:06:30.810
which allegedly showed
Rittenhouse assaulting a girl

00:06:30.810 --> 00:06:32.500
who was in a fight with her sister

00:06:32.500 --> 00:06:34.440
was also not relevant
to what Rittenhouse did

00:06:34.440 --> 00:06:36.640
on August 20th, 2020 in Kenosha.

00:06:36.640 --> 00:06:37.940
The video suggested Rittenhouse

00:06:37.940 --> 00:06:39.210
had a propensity for violence,

00:06:39.210 --> 00:06:41.750
but neither video had anything
to do with the protest.

00:06:41.750 --> 00:06:43.270
Now it's important to note that judges

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:45.050
are arbiters and are supposed to protect

00:06:45.050 --> 00:06:46.350
the rights of a criminal defendant.

00:06:46.350 --> 00:06:49.510
And certainly, these videos
make Rittenhouse look bad,

00:06:49.510 --> 00:06:52.120
but we aren't supposed to
convict people for looking bad.

00:06:52.120 --> 00:06:53.620
The judge was limiting the evidence

00:06:53.620 --> 00:06:55.060
so that the only evidence the jury heard

00:06:55.060 --> 00:06:57.840
had to do with what actually
happened on August 20th

00:06:57.840 --> 00:07:00.260
when Rittenhouse
encountered the three men.

00:07:00.260 --> 00:07:02.100
Now crim law 101 is that you can't,

00:07:02.100 --> 00:07:03.300
or at least generally you can't

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:05.450
because there are always exceptions,

00:07:05.450 --> 00:07:07.360
you can't use propensity evidence

00:07:07.360 --> 00:07:10.670
to show someone committed
a crime on a specific day.

00:07:10.670 --> 00:07:12.980
You can show that they
committed the crime at issue,

00:07:12.980 --> 00:07:14.360
but you're not allowed to convict them

00:07:14.360 --> 00:07:17.080
because they had a certain
propensity towards crime.

00:07:17.080 --> 00:07:18.530
Generally speaking, a criminal defendant

00:07:18.530 --> 00:07:20.360
can be convicted just
because they have a history

00:07:20.360 --> 00:07:21.400
of doing bad things.

00:07:21.400 --> 00:07:22.630
The state still has to prove

00:07:22.630 --> 00:07:23.690
that they committed the crimes

00:07:23.690 --> 00:07:24.750
they were charged with.

00:07:24.750 --> 00:07:26.567
And so when Judge Schroeder yelled,

00:07:26.567 --> 00:07:27.910
"Don't get brazen with me,"

00:07:27.910 --> 00:07:29.460
it was the culmination of two mistakes

00:07:29.460 --> 00:07:31.260
by the prosecutor
referring to Rittenhouse's

00:07:31.260 --> 00:07:33.630
post-arrest silence,
after being told not to,

00:07:33.630 --> 00:07:35.360
and referring to the CVS videotape,

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:37.240
despite being told not to.

00:07:37.240 --> 00:07:38.850
Binger felt that the
defense had opened the door

00:07:38.850 --> 00:07:41.150
and it was now okay to go
into that subject area.

00:07:41.150 --> 00:07:42.830
But even if that were the case,

00:07:42.830 --> 00:07:44.050
you still need to get permission

00:07:44.050 --> 00:07:46.100
from the judge or face their wrath.

00:07:46.100 --> 00:07:48.787
And Binger still had a
hard time letting it go.

00:07:48.787 --> 00:07:50.420
"This was the subject of emotion,

00:07:50.420 --> 00:07:51.480
I'm well aware of that.

00:07:51.480 --> 00:07:53.390
And the court left the door open."

00:07:53.390 --> 00:07:56.660
But Judge Schroeder screamed,
"For me, not for you."

00:07:56.660 --> 00:07:58.880
You should have come and
asked for reconsideration.

00:07:58.880 --> 00:08:00.890
All trial lawyers know that if you appear

00:08:00.890 --> 00:08:02.430
to disregard a judge's order,

00:08:02.430 --> 00:08:03.840
you're probably going to get it.

00:08:03.840 --> 00:08:04.950
And to the judge's credit,

00:08:04.950 --> 00:08:06.280
well, we all got to see Schroeder

00:08:06.280 --> 00:08:08.130
lambaste the prosecutors on the live feed.

00:08:08.130 --> 00:08:09.490
The jury was sequestered away

00:08:09.490 --> 00:08:11.320
and would have been completely unaware.

00:08:11.320 --> 00:08:14.110
So it wouldn't have prejudiced
the prosecution's case.

00:08:14.110 --> 00:08:15.720
That takes us to incident number five.

00:08:15.720 --> 00:08:17.010
Judge Schroeder allowed Rittenhouse

00:08:17.010 --> 00:08:19.490
to pick his own jurors?

00:08:19.490 --> 00:08:20.810
Well, not exactly.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:23.000
Judge Schroeder had
Rittenhouse blindly pick

00:08:23.000 --> 00:08:26.200
the 12 jurors who would be
deciding his case out of a hat.

00:08:26.200 --> 00:08:28.350
It's weird, but in
Wisconsin and other states,

00:08:28.350 --> 00:08:30.010
the courts regularly seat more jurors

00:08:30.010 --> 00:08:31.980
than necessary because
at the end of trials,

00:08:31.980 --> 00:08:34.380
it's common for jurors to
be stricken from the panel

00:08:34.380 --> 00:08:35.340
for various reasons.

00:08:35.340 --> 00:08:37.580
This could be a for misconduct

00:08:37.580 --> 00:08:39.610
or a juror having a family emergency

00:08:39.610 --> 00:08:40.670
or just getting sick.

00:08:40.670 --> 00:08:43.000
And the Rittenhouse trial,
18 perspective jurors

00:08:43.000 --> 00:08:43.880
heard all the evidence.

00:08:43.880 --> 00:08:45.050
After closing arguments,

00:08:45.050 --> 00:08:46.880
the judge told Rittenhouse
to randomly pick

00:08:46.880 --> 00:08:48.600
six numbers out of a tumbler.

00:08:48.600 --> 00:08:51.320
Those six numbers were
stricken from the panel.

00:08:51.320 --> 00:08:53.210
The 12 who were left were his jury.

00:08:53.210 --> 00:08:55.900
Whittling down the jurors is not unusual.

00:08:55.900 --> 00:08:58.860
Picking them out of a
hat is somewhat unusual.

00:08:58.860 --> 00:09:00.800
Judge Schroeder said he'd have defendants

00:09:00.800 --> 00:09:02.170
do the selection for years.

00:09:02.170 --> 00:09:04.280
He said that responding to critics,

00:09:04.280 --> 00:09:05.820
he started doing it after a case

00:09:05.820 --> 00:09:08.470
where there was only one
perspective black juror.

00:09:08.470 --> 00:09:11.280
That person was eliminated from the jury

00:09:11.280 --> 00:09:13.300
when the clerk picked that juror's name

00:09:13.300 --> 00:09:15.240
out of the panel.

00:09:15.240 --> 00:09:16.770
That resulted in a black defendant

00:09:16.770 --> 00:09:19.020
being tried by an all white jury

00:09:19.020 --> 00:09:21.460
as a result of one of
the court administrators.

00:09:21.460 --> 00:09:23.970
Judge Schroeder felt
that that was not ideal.

00:09:23.970 --> 00:09:25.930
He said, "It was a bad optic, I thought.

00:09:25.930 --> 00:09:28.350
I think people feel better
when they have control.

00:09:28.350 --> 00:09:29.930
So ever since that case,

00:09:29.930 --> 00:09:31.640
I have had an almost universal policy

00:09:31.640 --> 00:09:33.550
of having the defendant do the picks.

00:09:33.550 --> 00:09:36.180
When only one part of Judge
Schroeder's explanation

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:39.030
was cherry picked and then
circulated among social media,

00:09:39.030 --> 00:09:40.690
it looked terrible.

00:09:40.690 --> 00:09:42.940
But actually it seems like his explanation

00:09:42.940 --> 00:09:44.470
makes perfect sense.

00:09:44.470 --> 00:09:47.090
While this process is a
little bit theatrical,

00:09:47.090 --> 00:09:50.690
the reasons for doing so
actually empower the defendants.

00:09:50.690 --> 00:09:53.880
And it's hard to imagine that
there would be any prejudice

00:09:53.880 --> 00:09:56.190
to either the defense or the prosecution

00:09:56.190 --> 00:09:58.470
as a result of this particular foible.

00:09:58.470 --> 00:10:00.380
That takes us to incident number six.

00:10:00.380 --> 00:10:03.400
The judge disallowed
zooming in on a video.

00:10:03.400 --> 00:10:04.870
Well, not exactly.

00:10:04.870 --> 00:10:06.640
Judge Schroeder was mocked for sustaining

00:10:06.640 --> 00:10:08.120
a defense objection to zooming in

00:10:08.120 --> 00:10:10.070
on video footage via in iPad.

00:10:10.070 --> 00:10:12.150
This was presented as
either a nefarious plot

00:10:12.150 --> 00:10:13.510
between the defense lawyers and the judge,

00:10:13.510 --> 00:10:16.180
or is evidence that the judge
was old and out of touch.

00:10:16.180 --> 00:10:17.610
Now it's not that the judge said

00:10:17.610 --> 00:10:19.940
that they couldn't zoom in at all.

00:10:19.940 --> 00:10:21.420
The judge just ruled that the prosecution

00:10:21.420 --> 00:10:22.670
could zoom in on images

00:10:22.670 --> 00:10:24.250
as long as they had an expert testify

00:10:24.250 --> 00:10:27.500
as to how the pinch and zoom
worked on an Apple device,

00:10:27.500 --> 00:10:29.330
to make sure that that process

00:10:29.330 --> 00:10:31.570
wasn't adulterating the evidence.

00:10:31.570 --> 00:10:33.590
Now, the prosecution
couldn't produce an expert

00:10:33.590 --> 00:10:36.230
on short notice so the jury
wound up seeing the video

00:10:36.230 --> 00:10:38.410
on a Windows machine hooked up to a TV.

00:10:38.410 --> 00:10:41.410
Now this was kind of a
ticky-tacky objection

00:10:41.410 --> 00:10:43.850
by the defense, but in fairness,

00:10:43.850 --> 00:10:47.000
lawyers are required to
authenticate an item of evidence.

00:10:47.000 --> 00:10:49.130
And this applies to every piece
of evidence it's introduced,

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:50.280
including government records,

00:10:50.280 --> 00:10:53.010
business records, photographs,
video, audio, you name it.

00:10:53.010 --> 00:10:55.330
But look, the prosecution
has the burden of proof.

00:10:55.330 --> 00:10:56.960
So it's on them to make sure

00:10:56.960 --> 00:10:58.760
that what they're doing,
if they are changing

00:10:58.760 --> 00:11:00.450
the evidence in any way,

00:11:00.450 --> 00:11:02.550
isn't adulterating the
underlying evidence.

00:11:02.550 --> 00:11:04.470
And of course, there was also an issue

00:11:04.470 --> 00:11:08.020
with drone footage
where a file was smaller

00:11:08.020 --> 00:11:09.280
than it should have been.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:11.240
And that takes us to the next incident

00:11:11.240 --> 00:11:13.400
where the judge apparently asked the jury

00:11:13.400 --> 00:11:15.570
to applaud for a defense witness.

00:11:15.570 --> 00:11:16.580
Well, sort of.

00:11:16.580 --> 00:11:17.600
This one is interesting.

00:11:17.600 --> 00:11:19.760
On Veteran's Day, Judge
Schroeder decided to recognize

00:11:19.760 --> 00:11:22.267
anyone in the courtroom who
had served in the armed forces.

00:11:22.267 --> 00:11:24.090
"I just want to observe
that it's Veterans Day,

00:11:24.090 --> 00:11:25.470
and any veterans in the room?

00:11:25.470 --> 00:11:27.250
On the jury or anywhere else?"

00:11:27.250 --> 00:11:28.837
When no one responded, the judge said,

00:11:28.837 --> 00:11:31.270
"Well, that's unusual not to
have at least someone in here,

00:11:31.270 --> 00:11:34.010
but Dr. Black is, what branch?"

00:11:34.010 --> 00:11:36.250
He was referring to the
defense witness, John Black,

00:11:36.250 --> 00:11:38.120
who was about to take the stand.

00:11:38.120 --> 00:11:39.730
Black is a use of force expert

00:11:39.730 --> 00:11:41.890
who told the judge that
he had served in the army.

00:11:41.890 --> 00:11:42.723
The judge then said,

00:11:42.723 --> 00:11:44.500
"Okay, I think we can
have a round of applause

00:11:44.500 --> 00:11:46.800
to the people who have
served our country."

00:11:46.800 --> 00:11:48.080
The entire courtroom applauded,

00:11:48.080 --> 00:11:49.760
including members of the jury.

00:11:49.760 --> 00:11:52.750
This one was just sloppy
and completely unnecessary.

00:11:52.750 --> 00:11:54.850
It seemed have the unintended effect

00:11:54.850 --> 00:11:57.220
of bolstering the reputation of Dr. Black

00:11:57.220 --> 00:11:58.450
before he took the stand,

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:00.350
who happened to be a defense witness,

00:12:00.350 --> 00:12:01.890
but it was just completely unnecessary

00:12:01.890 --> 00:12:05.310
to run that risk for a
little grand standing

00:12:05.310 --> 00:12:06.520
in this particular case.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:07.670
And then there were accusations

00:12:07.670 --> 00:12:09.680
that Judge Schroeder was
just unusually chummy

00:12:09.680 --> 00:12:12.320
with Rittenhouse and wanted
to see him acquitted.

00:12:12.320 --> 00:12:15.540
For example, there's
a picture of the judge

00:12:15.540 --> 00:12:17.450
and Rittenhouse being very close together

00:12:17.450 --> 00:12:19.140
as they're watching a video.

00:12:19.140 --> 00:12:20.570
But equally likely is that Schroeder

00:12:20.570 --> 00:12:23.720
was just a relatively pro defendant judge.

00:12:23.720 --> 00:12:25.440
And of course there's a wide disparity

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:29.230
between criminal defendants
who are remanded to custody

00:12:29.230 --> 00:12:31.620
and those who are out on
their own recognizance.

00:12:31.620 --> 00:12:35.150
Rittenhouse was able to
attend, not in shackles,

00:12:35.150 --> 00:12:38.090
not going back to prison and in a suit.

00:12:38.090 --> 00:12:40.090
We don't wanna make things
too easy on the prosecutor.

00:12:40.090 --> 00:12:42.400
And maybe the answer here
is that criminal defendants

00:12:42.400 --> 00:12:44.480
more often should get
the benefit of the doubt

00:12:44.480 --> 00:12:46.070
like Rittenhouse did.

00:12:46.070 --> 00:12:48.270
But more pragmatically,
if a judge doesn't err

00:12:48.270 --> 00:12:49.670
on the side of the defense,

00:12:49.670 --> 00:12:51.110
they are likely to see an appeal

00:12:51.110 --> 00:12:53.670
and no trial judge wants
to, A, get reversed

00:12:53.670 --> 00:12:57.020
by the court of appeals, and
B, have to retry a defendant.

00:12:57.020 --> 00:12:59.900
And that's one reason why
they might err on the side

00:12:59.900 --> 00:13:03.490
of disallowing evidence
that might otherwise come in

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:05.160
to make sure that there aren't other

00:13:05.160 --> 00:13:06.620
constitutional violations.

00:13:06.620 --> 00:13:07.453
And for better or worse,

00:13:07.453 --> 00:13:08.910
Schroeder was clearly paying attention

00:13:08.910 --> 00:13:11.017
to how the trial was covered.

00:13:11.017 --> 00:13:12.650
"When I talked about
problems with the media,

00:13:12.650 --> 00:13:14.020
when this trial started,

00:13:14.020 --> 00:13:15.360
we're there in part, not fully,

00:13:15.360 --> 00:13:17.860
but in part, because of
grossly irresponsible handling

00:13:17.860 --> 00:13:19.120
of what comes out of this trial.

00:13:19.120 --> 00:13:20.190
I will tell you this,

00:13:20.190 --> 00:13:21.410
I'm going to think long and hard

00:13:21.410 --> 00:13:24.047
about live television of
a trial again, next time."

00:13:24.047 --> 00:13:26.710
"I don't know, I've always
been a firm believer in it

00:13:26.710 --> 00:13:29.340
because I think the people
should be able to see

00:13:29.340 --> 00:13:31.090
what's going on, but what I see,

00:13:31.090 --> 00:13:33.300
what's being done is
really quite frightening.

00:13:33.300 --> 00:13:35.030
Frightening, that's
the right word for it."

00:13:35.030 --> 00:13:37.820
Now I can't imagine how he
possibly got that impression,

00:13:37.820 --> 00:13:39.560
which takes us to the final incidents.

